<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=oral-rehydration-therapy">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oral rehydration therapy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oral rehydration therapy treats dehydration, especially from diarrhoea, with water containing modest amounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and salts; the earliest known use was reported by Hemendra Nath Chatterjee in The Lancet in 1953, for patients with mild cholera. It became common after the 1960s finding that glucose promotes the absorption of sodium and water during cholera. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh-liberation-war">Bangladesh Liberation War</a> of 1971, refugees with cholera given it had a mortality of 3.6%, against 30% with intravenous fluids. It is estimated to lower the risk of death from diarrhoea by up to 93%.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oral-rehydration-therapy.jpg" alt="Oral rehydration therapy" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Centers for Disease Control and Prevention · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cholera_rehydration_nurses.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1953</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Hemendra Nath Chatterjee</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>It came into wide use after the 1960s discovery that glucose speeds the absorption of sodium and water in cholera.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=oral-rehydration-therapy">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
